+## Self-review
+
+- Scope discipline: no caller of `extractInlineAssets`/`restoreInlineAssets`/`missingTokens` was touched; `reconcileInlineAssets` is additive and unreferenced by any caller yet (T17's job). `pathA.ts`, `pathB.ts`, `refine.ts`, `designAgentCli.ts`, `designAgent.ts` were not modified.
+- Only my own files were staged and committed (`git add <path>` by name, never `-A`/`.`); nothing under `.specclaw/**` or `.superpowers/**` was staged — confirmed via `git status --short` before and after each commit, and the pre-commit hook (lint-staged + eslint --fix + prettier) ran normally both times, no `--no-verify`.
+- Token regex: reused `TOKEN_PREFIX`/`TOKEN_SUFFIX`, the same constants `extractInlineAssets` mints tokens from — no second hardcoded literal pattern, per the ruling. `missingTokens`'s substring-based check was deliberately left as-is (different mechanism — `.includes()` per known token — since it's a distinct pre-existing function and rewiring is out of scope).
+- Return shape: I kept the ruling's exact three `kind` values (`clean` / `restored` / `mismatch`); `restored.missing` is a `string[]`, `mismatch.reason` is a human-readable string for logging (mirroring the file's existing style of "let callers log a warning" in `missingTokens`'s doc comment).
+- `sentTokens` typed as `string[]` (not derived automatically from an `assets` record) per ruling #2's "match existing call conventions" — the caller (T17) will pass `Object.keys(assets)`, same as `missingTokens`'s own key-list usage pattern, just supplied directly instead of re-deriving from a record inside this function.
+- Considered whether the lazy-wildcard `TOKEN_RE` could over-match arbitrary underscore-delimited text unrelated to real tokens (e.g. two unrelated `__..__` sequences with filler text between the prefix and the next `__`). Accepted this as intentional per the ruling's grouping of "renamed" with "unknown token" — a wrapper-shaped string is meant to be caught, not ignored. All 14 tests (7 pre-existing + 7 new) pass with this regex, including the byte-lossless round-trip test in the existing `extractInlineAssets`/`restoreInlineAssets` suite (those two functions don't use `TOKEN_RE` at all, so they were never at risk).
+- Both gates (typecheck, lint, unit) were run after each task, not just at the end, so each commit stands on its own as gate-clean.
+
+## Concerns
+
+- None blocking. One judgment call flagged above (T10: no strict RED/GREEN cycle since it's pure prompt text, not driven behavior — the test was written to match content I'd already written, then run once and passed). If the controller wants a stricter TDD trail for T10 specifically, say so and I'll redo it (write the assertions against the _old_ note text first to get a real RED, then apply the edit).
+- The "renamed" test case's exact string (`__INLINE_ASSET_RENAMED__`) is one instance of "renamed"; I didn't exhaustively test every conceivable renamed shape (e.g. a token that drops the trailing `__` suffix entirely, which would not match `TOKEN_RE` at all and would look like a clean absence rather than a mismatch — arguably correct, since without the suffix wrapper there's no recognizable token-shaped string left to distrust). This boundary wasn't in the brief's explicit test list, so I left it as an implicit consequence of the regex design rather than adding a test for it.
+
